{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnYTvEdgDaHKTegugUhP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've found that the layernorm weights for pre_feedforward_layernorm are exactly −1 in some dimensions in certain layers, so the 1+w term hits zero and would divide by zero. Those dimensions get zeroed out at the MLP input, so I'll handle them by masking. I'm also noticing that the gate and up top-1 input directions match exactly (cosine 1.0) across layers 9–17, which fits a shared input direction, and I want to check how closely the gate top-1 directions align from layer to layer.\n\nNow I'm checking the cross-layer alignment for the gate top-1 directions and the q top-1 directions, which both line up strongly across layers 9 to 17. That suggests a shared residual direction, possibly just the mean residual across positions, acting like a bias term that would shift the gate and query outputs by a constant amount.\n\nI'm working out how the anchor direction could tell me whether a unit's gate pre-activation is pushed further from zero, since flipping the sign of the anchor flips both the change and the base value, leaving their product unchanged. The open question is where the real leverage sits, so I'm going to check the composition of the anchor by looking at its top dimensions.\n\nCopying through the OV circuit looked weak and inconsistent in the earlier runs, and the massive dimensions seem to dominate those measurements. I'm now wondering whether to measure copying on the content subspace instead, or in the readout space through the unembedding, and I'm starting to look at which dimensions carry the massive activations.\n\nI'm checking the final norm weights for the dimensions where the scale is near zero, since the massive residual dimensions would seem to be suppressed there, and I'll print the smallest values of 1+w for each norm.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11233,"cache_read_input_tokens":91727,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11233},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d212505-2370-4998-83f2-5c6798c04a82","uuid":"31471a12-f4c4-4df1-a090-9bb0058689e1","timestamp":"2026-11-06T23:29:30.149Z","request_id":"req_011CgnYTumbr1t2hZ6pueZj5","thinking_duration_ms":15322,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnYTvEdgDaHKTegugUhP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Run 11 found a clear shared direction.\n- In layers 9–17 the top input direction of Δgate and Δup is identical (|cos| 0.99–1.00) and agrees with Δq's top input direction (|cos| 0.5–0.7).\n- Across layers 9–17 these gate directions are mutually aligned at 0.6–0.99.\n\nSo a large part of A−B is rank-1 along a common residual direction x̄: the gradient structure Σδᵢ·x̄ᵀ expected when the trained positions share a dominant residual component. That is the answer-position/template residual or a constant (\"bias-like\") direction. A−B therefore changes the effective biases of many units and heads along x̄.\n\nThe divide-by-zero also shows that some pre_feedforward_layernorm weights equal −1 exactly, i.e. those residual dims are switched off for the MLP. P(A sycophantic) ≈ 0.5.\n\nBefore using x̄, I need to know what it is. Is it the massive-activation dimension(s) of the Gemma residual? Those are signalled by norm weights near −1 (the scale (1+w) is ~0, so the dim is muted) or by extreme final-norm weights. Or is it a spread-out content direction? I'll list the special dimensions of each norm and the composition of the shared direction x̄. This is diagnostic and favours neither model.\n\n```python\ndef L(side, k): return np.load(f\"/work/{side}/{k}.npy\")\nfor l in range(18):\n    s = []\n    for n in [\"input_layernorm\",\"pre_feedforward_layernorm\",\"post_attention_layernorm\",\"post_feedforward_layernorm\"]:\n        w = 1 + L(\"A\", f\"model.layers.{l}.{n}.weight\")\n        small = np.where(np.abs(w) < 0.05)[0]\n        s.append(f\"{n[:8]} near0 {list(small[:8])} n={len(small)}\")\n    print(l, \" | \".join(s))\nwf = 1 + L(\"A\", \"model.norm.weight\")\nprint(\"final norm: smallest\", np.argsort(np.abs(wf))[:10], np.sort(np.abs(wf))[:10])\nprint(\"final norm: largest\", np.argsort(-wf)[:10], -np.sort(-wf)[:10])\n# shared gate direction (raw, and in normalized coords with safe division)\nfor l in [9, 12, 15, 17]:\n    dff = 1 + L(\"A\", f\"model.layers.{l}.pre_feedforward_layernorm.weight\")\n    D = L(\"A\", f\"model.layers.{l}.mlp.gate_proj.weight\") - L(\"B\", f\"model.layers.{l}.mlp.gate_proj.weight\")\n    U, S, Vt = np.linalg.svd(D, full_matrices=False)\n    v = Vt[0]\n    i = np.argsort(-np.abs(v))[:10]\n    print(l, \"gate top1 raw dims\", i, v[i].round(3), \"PR\", (1/np.sum(v**4)).round(1), \"S\", S[:4].round(3))\n    vn = np.where(np.abs(dff) > 1e-3, v/np.where(np.abs(dff)>1e-3, dff, 1), 0); vn /= np.linalg.norm(vn)\n    i = np.argsort(-np.abs(vn))[:10]\n    print(\"   normalized dims\", i, vn[i].round(3), \"PR\", (1/np.sum(vn**4)).round(1))\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11233,"cache_read_input_tokens":91727,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11233},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d212505-2370-4998-83f2-5c6798c04a82","uuid":"9507e8d2-8362-4d91-b720-2992f4ac46a7","timestamp":"2026-11-06T23:29:39.038Z","request_id":"req_011CgnYTumbr1t2hZ6pueZj5"}
